# Bytebase Setup Guide — {DB_NAME}

This is a **one-time, console-side setup**. None of it can be scripted from
this repo, because it's state that lives inside Bytebase itself, not in git.
Do this once per Bytebase workspace (not once per environment) — the actual
per-environment Postgres provisioning already happened when you ran
`bootstrap/{BOOTSTRAP_FILE}` for each environment (see the main README).

Do the steps below **after** the Postgres bootstrap has succeeded for every
environment — Bytebase needs a working, connectable database to register.

## Prerequisites

- A self-hosted Bytebase instance reachable from wherever Jenkins runs.
  ```bash
  docker run --rm --init \
    --name bytebase \
    --publish 8080:8080 --pull always \
    --volume ~/.bytebase/data:/var/opt/bytebase \
    bytebase/bytebase:{BYTEBASE_VERSION}
  ```
  For production, run this on infrastructure Jenkins' network can reach —
  never expose it directly to the public internet.
- The `{DB_NAME}` bootstrap already applied for every environment
  ({ENV_LIST}), so each Postgres instance already has the `{DB_NAME}` owner
  role, schemas, and grants in place.

## Step 1 — Create environments

**Settings → Environments** → create one environment per deployment target,
named to match this repo exactly: {ENV_LIST}. The names matter — the
Jenkinsfile's `--target-stage=environments/<name>` flag must match verbatim.

## Step 2 — Register each Postgres instance

**Instances → Add Instance**, once per environment:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Environment | the matching one from Step 1 |
| Host / Port | your Postgres host for that environment |
| Username | `{DB_NAME}` |
| Password | the `OWNER_PASSWORD` you set for that environment in `.env` |
| Database | `{DB_NAME}` |

Bytebase connects **as the owner role**, because that's the credential that
holds DDL rights (`CREATE`/`ALTER`/`DROP` on the schemas it owns). This is
why the owner role — and only the owner role — is `LOGIN`-enabled with a
password Bytebase is trusted with; treat this credential like a production
secret, store it only in Bytebase's own secret storage and in Jenkins
credentials, never in a shared team password doc.

## Step 3 — Create the project and add the databases

**Projects → New Project** → name it `{DB_NAME}` (or your own convention —
just update `BYTEBASE_PROJECT` in the Jenkinsfile to match). Add the
`{DB_NAME}` database from every instance you registered in Step 2 to this
project.

Once databases are added, note the exact resource name Bytebase assigns each
one (visible in the database detail page URL or via the API) — it will look
like `instances/<instance-id>/databases/{DB_NAME}`. You need these for
Step 6.

## Step 4 — Create the CI/CD service account

**IAM & Admin → Users & Groups → Add User**:

- Name: `svc-jenkins-{DB_NAME}` (or your convention)
- Roles: `Workspace Member` + `GitOps Service Agent`

Open the new account and **Copy Service Key** — this is the
`BYTEBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_SECRET`. You will not be able to view it again
after leaving the page.

## Step 5 — Configure SQL Review

**CI/CD → SQL Review → Create SQL Review** → start from the Sample Template
→ attach it to your production-equivalent environment(s) from Step 1 (all
environments after the first one in {ENV_LIST}, at minimum). This is what
makes `bytebase-action check` actually catch risky DDL in the Jenkins
pipeline instead of rubber-stamping everything.

## Step 6 — Wire up Jenkins

This repo uses a **branch-per-environment** model: `{BRANCH_MAP_SUMMARY}`.
Each of those branches deploys to exactly one environment — there's no
sequential promotion through every environment on a single push. Promotion
happens in git, by merging one environment branch into the next (e.g.
merging `development` into `main`), which is also where your normal PR
review already lives.

1. **Create a Multibranch Pipeline job** (not a plain Pipeline job) pointed
   at this repo — this is what makes Jenkins run a separate build per
   branch and populates `env.BRANCH_NAME`, which the Jenkinsfile relies on
   to look up which environment (if any) a branch deploys to.
2. **Manage Jenkins → Credentials** — add two **Secret text** credentials:
   - ID `bytebase-service-account` → value: the service account's email
   - ID `bytebase-service-account-secret` → value: the service key from Step 4
3. Open the generated `Jenkinsfile` and replace the `TODO` placeholders:
   - `BYTEBASE_URL` → your Bytebase instance URL
   - `BYTEBASE_PROJECT` → `projects/{DB_NAME}` (confirm the exact name from Step 3)
   - Each `target:` value inside `BRANCH_ENV_MAP` → the resource names you
     noted in Step 3, e.g. `instances/dev-instance/databases/{DB_NAME}`
4. Optionally set **required approvers / wait timer** at the environment
   level in Bytebase's own **Rollout Policy** settings (Enterprise plan) —
   independent of, and in addition to, the `requireApproval` flag already
   set per-branch in the generated `BRANCH_ENV_MAP` (which pauses the
   Jenkins *pipeline* on an `input` step; Bytebase's rollout policy can
   independently require sign-off on the *rollout issue* itself even if
   something calls the API directly).

## Verifying it works

1. Add a small migration under `migrations/`, e.g.
   `202607230001_create_example_table_ddl.sql`, on a feature branch off of
   your dev branch.
2. Open a PR into the branch mapped to your dev environment — the
   `SQL Review (check)` stage runs on every branch and reports
   warnings/errors.
3. Merge it — Jenkins triggers a build for that branch, runs the check
   again, then rolls out automatically (no approval gate on the
   lowest-stakes environment by default).
4. When ready to promote, open a PR merging that branch into the next one
   in `{BRANCH_MAP_SUMMARY}` (e.g. `development` → `main`). Merging triggers
   a build on the target branch, which pauses on Jenkins' `input` step
   (`requireApproval: true` for every environment after the first) before
   rolling out.
5. Confirm in Bytebase's **Issues** view that a release + rollout was
   created for the right environment, and that the migration actually
   landed in the target database.

## Testing locally, without Jenkins

You don't need Jenkins running to test any of this. Two pieces make that
possible:

**A disposable local Bytebase**, if you don't want to point at a shared
instance while testing:
```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.bytebase.yml up -d
# open http://localhost:8080, create a workspace admin, then repeat
# steps 2-5 above against it
```

**Running the exact same commands Jenkins would, by hand:**
```bash
scripts/bytebase-manual-rollout.sh --env dev --check     # SQL Review only, read-only, safe anytime
scripts/bytebase-manual-rollout.sh --env dev --rollout   # actually deploys, asks for confirmation first
```
This calls the same `bytebase-action` Docker image with the same flags the
`Jenkinsfile` uses — it's a faithful local reproduction, not a separate
path that could drift out of sync, and the env→target mapping comes from
the same `--environments`/`--branch-map` values used when this repo was
generated. Use `--check` freely; `--rollout` actually creates a release and
deploys, so it prompts before doing anything.

